  "query_preview": "..\n\nA challenging forecast continues to plague the Great Lakes Region.\nA hot and humid air mass with a high degree of instability is in \nplace this afternoon. Temperatures have reached into the upper 80s \nto 90 degrees as of 2 PM, with heat indices well into the 90s. A \ncouple more hours left to increase those values as well. The broken \nline of convection which was just south of the border this morning \nwithin the northern edge of the 850-700 MB Theta-E ridge has crept \nnorthward into southwest lower Michigan and will continue to drift \nnorth, presenting a opportunity for showers and thunderstorms this \nevening and tonight. The highest instability and wind shear are \nactually located over center/northern Lower Michigan this afternoon \nper SPC mesoscale analysis, making areas north of M-46 probably the \ngreatest concern for isolated severe storm, but the entire CWA has a \nMarginal Risk through tonight. Once again, hi-res solutions continue \nto underestimate the coverage of showers and thunderstorms. \n\nA couple opportunities exist for ridge runners as upper level \nimpulses eject out of the central/northern Plains both Wednesday and \nThursday. Growing consensus points to a shortwave tracking through \nnorthern lower Michigan tomorrow (15-19z), with a surface \nfront/reflection along or north of I-69 providing the focus. Even \nwith this potential earlier timing, there will most likely be \nsufficient instability around to support severe storms alongside \nhigh 0-6 KM bulk shear values aoa 50 knots. A Slight Risk of severe \nweather remains in place for both large hail and damaging winds. \nMachine learning even highlights the northern Thumb region for a \ntornado threat, which is supported by 0-1 KM bulk shear values \naround 25 knots. If this faster timing materializes, max \ntemperatures could come up short of the outgoing forecast in the 90-\n94 degree range; thus, confidence is not high enough to issue a Heat \nAdvisory, as peak values in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees \ncould be short-lived once again. \n\nYet another upper level impulse will race through Wednesday night or \nThursday morning. Coupled with nocturnal low level jet enhancement, \nstrong thunderstorms producing heavy rain become a concern. \nAlthough, at this time, there is no indication of training. None-the-\nless, PW Values aoa 1.75 inches and 700 MB dew pts still locally \nnearing 5 C will certainly be supportive of locally heavy rain, with \nanother opportunity or two between Thursday night and Friday night. \nConfidence remains low, however. Longwave pattern guidance for the \nweekend indicates upper level confluent flow and subsequent high \npressure building in, trending the forecast in a drier direction.",
